'''Jay Robert Pritzker''' (born January 19, 1965)<ref>https://www.britannica.com/biography/J-B-Pritzker</ref> is an American politician, philanthropist, and businessman serving since 2019 as the 43rd [[governor of Illinois]]. He is a member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite news |date=2025-12-15 |title=The Democrat testing the appetite for a leader from the 'one percent' |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2025/12/15/billionaires-pritzker-democrats-president-trump/ |access-date=2026-02-19 |work=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

The [[Pritzker family]] owns the [[Hyatt]] hotel chain, and JB Pritzker helped create several [[Chicago]]-based venture capital and investment startups. He was also active in politics decades before serving in elected office. After an unsuccessful [[1998 United States House of Representatives elections in Illinois|1998 bid]] for [[Illinois's 9th congressional district]], Pritzker became a major financial supporter of candidates and served as a national co-chair of [[Hillary Clinton]]'s [[Hillary Clinton 2008 presidential campaign|2008 presidential campaign]]. According to ''[[Forbes]]'', as of August 2025, his estimated net worth is $3.9 billion. This makes him the second-wealthiest U.S. elected official, behind President [[Donald Trump]].

Pritzker won a crowded Democratic primary for governor of Illinois in the [[2018 Illinois gubernatorial election|2018 election]] and defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[Bruce Rauner]] in the general election. He was reelected in [[2022 Illinois gubernatorial election|2022]], defeating [[Darren Bailey]]. A [[Progressivism in the United States|progressive]] Democrat, he has [[Legality of cannabis by U.S. jurisdiction|legalized recreational cannabis]], raised the [[Minimum wage in the United States|minimum wage]], expanded [[Abortion in the United States|abortion]] access, and unsuccessfully sought to implement [[Illinois Fair Tax|income tax reforms]]. During his second term, Pritzker has become a prominent critic of the [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|second Trump administration]].

